/* data/learn-c/phy-11-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-05"] = {"5.1":{"s":"Gravitation is the attraction between any two masses. It explains why objects fall, why the Moon goes round the Earth and the planets go round the Sun. This chapter covers Kepler's laws of planetary motion, Newton's law of gravitation, how g changes with height, depth and latitude, gravitational potential energy, escape speed and the motion of satellites."},"5.2":{"s":"Kepler's three laws describe planetary motion. First: a planet moves in an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. Second: the line from the Sun to the planet sweeps equal areas in equal times, so a planet moves faster when it is nearer the Sun; this follows from conservation of angular momentum. Third: the square of the period is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis.","f":[{"x":"Delta A/Delta t = L/(2*m) = constant","n":"Law of areas: L = angular momentum of the planet of mass m; the area swept per unit time is constant.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.2, p.79"},{"x":"T^2 proportional to r^3","n":"Law of periods: T = time for one revolution, r = semi-major axis. So T^2/r^3 is the same for all planets.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.2, p.80"}],"w":["In T² ∝ r³ the r is the SEMI-MAJOR axis, which is the average distance of the planet from the Sun."]},"5.3":{"s":"Newton's universal law of gravitation: the attraction between two particles grows with the product of their masses and falls as the square of their separation. The force acts along the line joining them, and the two forces form an action-reaction pair. G is the universal gravitational constant. A uniform hollow shell exerts no force on a mass inside it, and acts like a point mass at its centre for a mass outside.","f":[{"x":"F = G*m_1*m_2/r^2","n":"Gravitational force between two point masses m_1 and m_2 a distance r apart; G = 6.67 × 10^-11 N m^2 kg^-2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.3, p.82"}],"w":["Doubling the distance makes the force one fourth, not one half.","Inside a hollow uniform shell the net gravitational force is zero."]},"5.4":{"s":"The value of G cannot be found from Newton's law alone. It is measured with the Cavendish balance, in which two small masses hang on a light rod from a fine wire, and two large masses are brought near them. The tiny gravitational pull twists the wire a little. The twist is measured with a beam of light reflected from a small mirror on the wire, which gives G."},"5.5":{"s":"The Earth attracts every object with a force, and the acceleration this causes, the acceleration due to gravity g, is the force per unit mass. For a point on the Earth's surface all the Earth's mass can be treated as if it were at the centre. So g depends on the mass and radius of the Earth, and not on the mass of the falling object, which is why heavy and light bodies fall together.","f":[{"x":"g = G*M/R^2","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at the Earth's surface: M = mass and R = radius of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.5, p.85"}],"w":["g does not depend on the mass of the falling body."]},"5.6":{"s":"The value of g is not the same everywhere. It decreases with height above the surface and with depth below it, and it is least at the Earth's centre, where it is zero. It also changes with latitude: the Earth's rotation and its slightly flattened shape make g smallest at the equator and largest at the poles. For small heights and depths, the changes are close to linear.","f":[{"x":"g_h = g*(1 - 2*h/R)","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at a small height h above the surface (h much smaller than R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.86"},{"x":"g_d = g*(1 - d/R)","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at a depth d below the surface, with uniform density; zero at the centre (d = R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.87"},{"x":"g = (4/3)*pi*R*rho*G","n":"g at the surface in terms of the mean density rho of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.87"},{"x":"g' = g - R*omega^2*cos^2(theta)","n":"Effective g at latitude theta, allowing for the Earth's rotation at angular speed omega; the reduction is greatest at the equator, zero at the poles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.6, p.89"}],"w":["g falls with height as 1/r² but with depth in a straight line: g is greatest at the surface.","At the poles the rotation has no effect on g; at the equator it reduces g by Rω²."]},"5.7":{"s":"Gravitational potential energy of a mass m at distance r from the Earth's centre is minus GMm/r, taking it as zero at infinity. It is negative because the force is attractive. Near the surface, the gain in energy on rising a small height h is mgh. Gravitational potential is the potential energy per unit mass, minus GM/r. The escape speed is the least speed at which an object can leave the Earth for ever, about 11.2 km/s.","f":[{"x":"U(r) = -G*M*m/r","n":"Gravitational potential energy of mass m at distance r from mass M (zero at infinity).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.90"},{"x":"Delta U = m*g*h","n":"Gain in potential energy for a small height h above the surface (h much smaller than R).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.91"},{"x":"V = U/m = -G*M/r","n":"Gravitational potential at distance r from mass M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.91"},{"x":"v_e = sqrt(2*G*M/R) = sqrt(2*g*R)","n":"Escape speed from the surface of a planet of mass M and radius R; for the Earth 11.2 km/s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.7, p.92"}],"w":["Potential energy is negative: a bound system has total energy below zero.","The escape speed does not depend on the mass of the object that escapes."]},"5.8":{"s":"A satellite stays in orbit when the gravitational pull on it provides exactly the centripetal force needed for its circular motion. This fixes its critical (orbital) speed, which falls as the orbit gets higher and does not depend on the satellite's mass. The period increases with the radius of the orbit: T² is proportional to the cube of the orbit radius. Inside a satellite everything is in free fall, so astronauts feel weightless.","f":[{"x":"v_c = sqrt(G*M/(R + h))","n":"Critical (orbital) speed at height h above the surface; close to the surface v_c = sqrt(g*R) = 7.92 km/s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.93"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt((R + h)^3/(G*M))","n":"Period of a satellite in a circular orbit of radius R + h; T^2 is proportional to (R + h)^3.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.95"},{"x":"N = m*(g + a) (lift up), N = m*(g - a) (lift down)","n":"Apparent weight in a lift accelerating upward or downward with acceleration a; it is zero in free fall (a = g).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 5.8, p.95"}],"w":["Weightlessness in a satellite does not mean gravity is absent: the satellite and astronaut are both falling freely.","A satellite with speed below the critical speed spirals down, and above the escape speed it leaves the Earth."]}};
